# Why are some industries dominated by giants?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What are economies of scale? When is one firm cheapest — a natural monopoly?"
next: {"title":"Should natural monopolies be regulated or owned by the state?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/112-should-natural-monopolies-be-regulated.cast"}

## Two firms, one sky
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Airbus
      - Boeing
      - COMAC
    values:
      - 766
      - 348
      - 13
    value_labels: true
    bar_colors: same
    icons:
      - airplane
      - airplane
      - airplane
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 900
    y_label: Airliners delivered, 2024
    gap: 0.5
    label_size: 24
    box:
      x: 30
      'y': 150
      w: 600
      h: 510
sources:
    - id: deliv2024
      title: Shaking out the Airbus and Boeing 2024 delivery numbers
      authors: Cirium; company reports
      year: 2025
      finding: Airbus delivered 766 commercial aircraft in 2024; Boeing delivered 348, its output hit by a machinists' strike and safety reviews.
      url: https://www.cirium.com/thoughtcloud/shaking-out-the-airbus-and-boeing-2024-delivery-numbers/
    - id: comac2024
      title: How good is the COMAC C919?
      authors: Leeham News
      year: 2025
      finding: COMAC delivered about 13 C919s in 2024 (China Eastern 8, Air China 2, China Southern 3); COMAC does not publish official figures.
      url: https://leehamnews.com/?p=46387
    - id: b787
      title: A $32B tally, but Boeing's 787 costs don't bother Wall Street
      authors: The Herald (Everett)
      year: 2016
      finding: 'By the third quarter of 2015 Boeing had spent $32.2 billion on the 787: $28.3 billion in deferred production costs and $3.9 billion in non-recurring costs such as tooling.'
      url: https://www.heraldnet.com/?p=29632

    text two "2 firms" #b5482e cites ["deliv2024"] x 800 y 520 font_size 56 hidden true
    icon bread size 130 set twemoji x 800 y 470 icon_look picture of bread hidden true
    text dozens "dozens per city" x 800 y 380 font_size 40 hidden true
    text merger "1997: Boeing + McDonnell Douglas" x 800 y 500 width 340 font_size 36 hidden true
    text bill "$32 billion" #b5482e cites ["b787"] x 800 y 520 font_size 64 hidden true
    text bill_cap "Boeing 787, by 2015" x 800 y 450 font_size 30 hidden true
    text bill_note "mostly: the costly first planes" #8f887c cites ["b787"] x 800 y 400 font_size 26 hidden true
    text oven "Bakery: an oven" x 800 y 330 font_size 36 hidden true
    text jet "Jet: billions" #b5482e x 800 y 270 font_size 36 hidden true
    text q "Cheapest size ÷ market size" #2f6b8f x 800 y 330 width 330 font_size 38 hidden true

    card title "Two firms, one sky"

Why are some industries run by a couple of giants, while others have thousands of small firms? Start with who builds the world's big passenger jets.
    draw axes

B: In  (@draw bar_1@)2024 Airbus delivered seven hundred and sixty-six of them.

    ask "Drag the next bar to how many Boeing delivered." intro "Your guess." on bar_2 store g default 700 relative true voice b
        right: Three hundred and forty-eight: close to your {g}.
        wrong: You said {g}: it was three hundred and forty-eight.

Three hundred and forty-eight, in a bad year of strikes and safet (@draw two@)y checks. Two firms build nearly all the world's large airliners.

B: A t (@draw bar_3@)hird, China's state-backed COMAC, delivered about thirteen.

    erase two

Now set that beside b (@draw bread dozens@)read. Any city has dozens of bakeries, and none of them bakes for the whole world.

    erase bread dozens

B: The naive answer: the giants crushed or bought their rivals. Boe (@draw merger@)ing did swallow McDonnell Douglas in 1997.

    erase merger

Sometimes that's the story. But look at the bill for a new jet. By 2015 B (@draw bill bill_cap@)oeing had put about thirty-two billion dollars into its 787.

Most of that was the  (@draw bill_note@)extra cost of the first planes, which came out far dearer to build than later ones.

B: A bakery needs an (@draw oven@) oven and a lease. A jet maker spends billions desi (@draw jet@)gning and tooling up, and more on those costly first planes, then must sell hundreds to earn it back.

    erase oven jet bill_note

So the question is how big a firm must be to (@draw q@) be cheap, next to how big its market is. And there's a twis (@highlight q effect underline@)t: some firms choose how big that has to be.

    pause 0.4

## How many firms fit?
domain: {"x":[0,1600],"y":[0,20]}
vars:
    F:
      value: 200
      min: 50
      max: 12000
      step: 50
      label: Setup cost ($ a day)
    Mk:
      value: 1000
      min: 200
      max: 1600
      step: 50
      label: Market size (a day)
    mes:
      expr: sqrt(200*F)
    'n':
      expr: Mk/sqrt(200*F)

    axes axes x_label "Output a day (Q)" y_label "Average cost ($)" cross corner ticks.x [400,800,1200] ticks.y [5,10,15,20] hidden true
    curve ATC #8a5fa8 expr "1 + F/x + x/200" x_from 12.5 x_to 1600 bind.x_from F/16 hidden true
    label label_ATC "LRAC" right #8a5fa8 attach_to ATC hidden true
    curve flatline #8f887c expr "3" x_from 200 x_to 1600 dash true hidden true
    label label_flatline "or flat?" right #8f887c attach_to flatline hidden true
    text boatlbl "Boat work: the market" #2f6b8f at.data [1000,1.6] font_size 26 hidden true
    dot mesp #8a5fa8 at.on ATC at.x 200 bind {"at.x":"mes"} hidden true
    label label_mes "MES" below #8a5fa8 attach_to mesp hidden true
    curve market #2f6b8f x_expr Mk y_expr t t_from 0 t_to 18.5 dash true hidden true
    label label_market "Market" above #2f6b8f attach_to market hidden true
    text readout "Market ÷ MES = {n:1}" #2f6b8f at.data [1230,4] font_size 38 hidden true
    text nm "Natural monopoly" #b5482e at.data [1290,10.5] font_size 40 hidden true
    dot nmp #b5482e at.on ATC at.x 1000 bind {"at.x":"Mk"} hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch math work [{"tex":"LRAC = c + \\frac{F}{Q} + bQ"},{"tex":"\\text{bottom: } \\frac{F}{Q^2} = b \\Rightarrow Q_{MES} = \\sqrt{F/b}"},{"tex":"F \\times 50 \\Rightarrow Q_{MES} \\times \\sqrt{50} \\approx 7"},{"tex":"\\text{firms} \\approx \\text{market} \\div Q_{MES}"}] x 500 y 400 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "How many firms fit?"

B: To see how many firms a market can feed, draw one firm's long-run average cost, LRAC: what each unit costs at each output, once it can choose any size.
    draw axes

B: A ba (@draw ATC label_ATC@)kery, with illustrative numbers.

B: It falls at first, as the oven and the lease are spread over more loaves. Past some point it stops falling: (@draw flatline label_flatline@) it may turn up, or just stay flat.
    highlight ATC effect pulse

So where's the sweet spot?
    point at.data [200,3] gesture circle

B: Where it stops falling: (@draw mesp label_mes@) here, two hundred loaves a day. That's the minimum efficien (@highlight label_mes effect underline@)t scale, the smallest output at which a firm is as cheap as it gets.

    erase flatline label_flatline

And the town eats,  (@draw market label_market@)say, a thousand loaves a day at that price.

So there's room f (@draw readout@)or five bakeries, each at its cheapest.

    erase label_market

    hide readout

B: Now the bo (@draw boatlbl@)atyard. Count its output in hours of boat work: the town needs, say, eight hundred a day.
    animate Mk 800 duration 2

B: And its slipway and crane cost far more than an oven. Where will the bottom of the bowl go?
    highlight ATC effect glow

    quiz "When the setup cost rises, where does the bottom of the bowl move?" judge false voice b
        right: Let's see.
        * Left
        * Right
        * Stays put

B: To the right. A bigger setup cost needs more output to spread it over, and here the bottom moves past the whole market.
    animate F 10000 duration 3.5 ghost.dash true

So where the market runs (@draw nmp@) out, the cost is still falling. One yard servin (@show readout@)g ever (@point at.ref nmp gesture circle@)y boat is cheaper than two splitting them.

B: That's a natural mo (@draw nm@)nopoly: one firm is the cheapest way to serve the whole ma (@highlight market label_market effect pulse@)rket. What to do about one is a question of its own.

B: Try it: slide the setup cost and the market's size, and watch how many firms fit.
    explore params ["F","Mk"]

So the number of firms isn't set by greed alone. Roughly, it's the market's size divided by the minimum efficient scale.
    highlight readout effect box

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want to see where the bottom of the bowl sits?

@math

    erase mathq

    fade axes ATC label_ATC mesp label_mes market boatlbl readout nm nmp to 0.15

Average cost is a unit cost, plus the setup cost F spread over Q, plus a part that rises with Q.
    draw math_box math_line_1

It bottoms out where the falling part and the rising part balance: at the square root of F over b.
    draw math_line_2

So fifty times the setup cost moves the minimum efficient scale only about seven times further out.
    draw math_line_3

And the firms that fit are roughly the market's size over that scale.
    draw math_line_4

    erase math

@test

    erase mathq

    fade axes ATC label_ATC mesp label_mes market boatlbl readout nm nmp to 1

    pause 0.3

## The ticket that grows
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - 0
      - 1
      - 2
      - 3
      - 4
    series:
      - {name: Plain goods, values: [60, 38, 24, 15, 10]}
      - {name: Heavily advertised, values: [60, 52, 47, 45, 45]}
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 70
    xlim:
      - 0
      - 4
    x_label: 'Market size: doublings (illustrative)'
    y_label: Top 4 firms' share (%)
    label_size: 24
    box:
      x: 40
      'y': 150
      w: 560
      h: 500
sources:
    - id: sutton1991
      title: 'Sunk Costs and Market Structure: Price Competition, Advertising, and the Evolution of Concentration'
      authors: John Sutton
      year: 1991
      finding: Across twenty food and drink markets in six countries (France, Germany, Italy, Japan, the UK, the US), the lower bound to concentration falls toward zero as markets grow where sunk costs are exogenous setup costs, but stays bounded away from zero where firms compete with endogenous sunk costs such as advertising; Sutton's Technology and Market Structure (1998) extends this to R&D.
    - id: grullon2019
      title: Are US Industries Becoming More Concentrated?
      authors: Gustavo Grullon, Yelena Larkin and Roni Michaely
      year: 2019
      finding: 'Since the late 1990s, over 75% of US industries have experienced an increase in concentration levels (Review of Finance 23(4): 697–743).'
      url: https://ideas.repec.org/a/oup/revfin/v23y2019i4p697-743..html
    - id: autor2020
      title: The Fall of the Labor Share and the Rise of Superstar Firms
      authors: David Autor, David Dorn, Lawrence F. Katz, Christina Patterson and John Van Reenen
      year: 2020
      finding: 'Sales have become more concentrated in US industries since 1982; concentration rose most in industries with faster growth of patent intensity and productivity, consistent with ''superstar'' firms winning by being more productive (Quarterly Journal of Economics 135(2): 645–709).'
      url: https://www.nber.org/digest/aug17/w23396.shtml
    - id: cgp2019
      title: From Good to Bad Concentration? U.S. Industries over the Past 30 Years
      authors: Matias Covarrubias, Germán Gutiérrez and Thomas Philippon
      year: 2019
      finding: Concentration in the 1990s looks efficient (tougher price competition, intangible investment, more productive leaders); after 2000 it looks more like inefficient concentration, with weaker competition and higher barriers to entry (NBER Macroeconomics Annual 34).
      url: https://nber.org/papers/w25983

    text why "Jets? Soft drinks?" #b5482e x 850 y 560 width 260 font_size 36 hidden true
    text chosen "Costs firms choose: ads, R&D" #2f6b8f cites ["sutton1991"] x 885 y 615 width 210 font_size 32 hidden true
    text sunk "Sunk: never got back" x 850 y 440 width 260 font_size 36 hidden true
    text rd "Jets: the R&D race" #b5482e x 850 y 560 width 260 font_size 36 hidden true
    text big "75%" #b5482e cites ["grullon2019"] x 500 y 550 font_size 96 hidden true
    text big_cap "of US industries: more concentrated since the late 1990s" x 500 y 470 width 820 font_size 34 hidden true
    text better "Better: superstars" #2f6b8f cites ["autor2020"] x 270 y 370 font_size 40 hidden true
    text protected "Protected: barriers" #b5482e cites ["cgp2019"] x 730 y 370 font_size 40 hidden true
    text s1 "How many fit: mostly agreed" x 500 y 260 font_size 38 hidden true
    text s2 "Better or protected: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 205 font_size 38 hidden true
    cards sorter deck true check each hidden true
        Fills with rivals: Hair salons
        Keeps its giants: Breakfast cereals
        Fills with rivals: Plumbers
        Keeps its giants: Microchips
        Fills with rivals: Cafés
        Keeps its giants: Vaccines
    scratch close work ["cheapest size: big next to the market","and firms can make it bigger"] x 500 y 420 font_size 38 hidden true

    card title "The ticket that grows"

If the number (@draw axes line_1@) of firms is market over minimum scale, a bigger market should hold more of them. Double the market, double the bakeries.

B: So as jets and soft dri (@draw why@)nks went global, why didn't they fill up with rivals?

    erase why

John Sutton's answer, in 1991: some setup costs aren't fixe (@draw chosen@)d by technology. Firms choose them: advertising, research.

B: The bigger the market, the bigger the prize for being best, so rivals spend more to win it. And once spen (@draw sunk@)t, it can't be got back: it's sunk.

    erase sunk

B: Your turn. For a plain good like flour, the top firms' share falls as the market grows.
    fade line_1 to 0.35

    ask "Draw the line for a heavily advertised good." on line_2 from 1 store g voice b
        right: It hardly falls: well drawn.
        wrong: Here's the line, beside yours: here, it hardly falls.

It barely falls. In twenty food and drink markets across six countries, Sutton found the floor under concentration sank with market size for plain goods, but stayed high where advertising raced.
    highlight line_2 effect glow

    erase chosen

B: Jets are th (@draw rd@)e research version: each new model must beat the last, so the entry ticket grows with the prize.

    erase axes line_1 line_2 rd

So are giants a sign of efficiency or of power? Since the late  (@draw big big_cap@)1990s, concentration has risen in more than three quarters of US industries.

B: David Autor and colleagues  (@draw better@)see superstar firms: concentration rose most where patents and productivity grew fastest. Big because better.

Thomas Philippon and colleagues see two eras (@draw protected@): good concentration in the 1990s, then, after 2000, weaker competition and higher barriers to entry.

B: How sure are we? That marke (@draw s1@)t size and setup costs shape how many firms fit: mostly agreed.

B: Whether today's giants (@draw s2@) are mostly better or mostly protected: contested.

    erase big big_cap better protected

    hide s1 s2

    draw sorter

    ask "As each market grows: rivals, or giants? Sort each card." intro "Three new cases." on sorter store f
        right: Salons, plumbers and cafés fill up; cereals, chips and vaccines keep their giants.
        wrong: Ask whether winners must keep outspending each other on ads or research: here, cereals, chips and vaccines keep their giants.

    erase sorter

    show s1 s2

    quiz "Which of these is most likely a natural monopoly?" voice b
        right: The water pipes: a second set would double a huge cost to serve the same homes.
        wrong: Look for the setup cost a second firm would have to copy in full: here, the pipes.
        + A town's water pipes
        * A town's restaurants
        * A town's taxis

    quiz "Two firms now sell 80% of a market. Which sign points to power rather than efficiency?"
        right: Rising profits with falling entry and investment: the giants are guarding the door, not getting better.
        wrong: Efficient giants grow by doing more for buyers; protected ones earn more while rivals stay out: here, profits up, entry down.
        * Prices fell and productivity rose
        + Profits rose while entry and investment fell
        * Both spend heavily on research

    erase s1 s2

So why are some industries dominated by giants? Because th (@draw close_box close_line_1@)e cheapest size is large next to the market, and sometimes firms  (@draw close_line_2@)keep making it larger.

Where each user makes a product better for the next, they grow bigger still: platforms, a story of their own.
    highlight close effect box

    pause 0.6

## Where next
